  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\n...UPDATED AVIATION/MARINE/BEACHES DISCUSSION...\n\nSatellite shows upper level clouds streaming through the region, \nsourced from moisture from Hurricane Odalys hundreds of miles to the \nsouth-southwest and carried through the jet stream. The 00z 9/26 \nsounding shows a shallow moist profile at around 25,000 feet, which \nis expected to deepen down to around 10,000 feet by this afternoon. \n\nToday through Monday...Despite the near surface remaining drier, \nPWAT's today will increase to around 1.50\" for San Diego County and \ndeserts east of the mountains, enough for extensive cloud cover \nthroughout the day but not enough for precipitation without any \nbackground instability or forcing. As a result, precip chances above \n10% have been reduced to southeastern portions of San Diego County \nand other mountain peaks from this afternoon through early Monday \nmorning. Elsewhere, virga showers aloft may bring some sprinkles to \nbriefly reach the ground. Because of the cloud cover region-wide, \nhigh temperatures will be noticeably cooler today relative to \nyesterday, around 6-12 degrees cooler. A sharp boundary of drier air \naloft moves through SoCal during the day Monday, out ahead of the \ntrough axis, which barely dips south enough to bring cooler \ntemperatures aloft and thus cooler daytime temperatures despite \nincreasing sunshine. Monday will be the coolest day of the week by \nfar with highs 75-85 degrees across the coastal basin, 60s for the \nmountains and mid-90s in the lower deserts. With the troughs gradual \npassage in the afternoon, onshore winds will be breezy east of \nmountain passes and along desert slopes (25-35 mph, higher through \nBanning Pass) and the marine later will build into the evening, with \nlow clouds likely Monday night for much of the coastal basin. \n\nTuesday...With the trough axis moving east, a weak offshore gradient \nbetween the Coastal Basin and the High Desert coupled with modest \nnortherly flow aloft will bring a decent (60%) chance for weak \nnortherly offshore flow. This would noticeably drop the relative \nhumidity Monday night into Tuesday for the deserts, mountains and \nInland Empire (especially near the Cajon Pass). High temperatures \nincrease noticeably to mid-80s to low-90s across the Coastal Basin \nwith perhaps a few low 80s at the coast. \n\nWednesday through the weekend...While offshore winds dissipate \nWednesday, daytime temperatures increase through the end of the week \nwhile relative humidities generally continue to decrease for inland \nareas. A ridge will be slowly developing out in the Pacific, moving \ntoward the southwest US through the end of the week.\n\nBy the end of the week, daytime RH's drop to 15-25% for elevated \ninterior valleys, the Inland Empire, mountains and deserts (locally \ndown to 10% in the High Desert) while temperatures reach into the \nmid-90s to low-100s in these areas. Even the lowland urban areas \njust a few miles inland of the coast will likely (50-80% chance) \nreach at least into the upper 80s and perhaps (20-60% chance) above \n90F for Thurs/Fri/Sat.",
